  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively point in the right direction: R2 and R3 show that Cr-containing BCC refractory HEAs can precipitate Cr-rich multicomponent Laves phases, while R6 and R8 supply the solid-solution/lattice-distortion strengthening mechanism. R7, R18, and R19 further suggest that controlling the topology and continuity of a second phase within a ductile matrix can improve the strength–ductility balance. Records such as R4, R5, and R12 also establish compositional alloying, arc melting of closely related Hf–Ta–Ti–Zr refractory alloys, and Cr alloying as plausible design tools. However, none identifies adding approximately 4.2 at.% Cr specifically to HfMoTaTiZr, predicts the reticulated Cr2(Ti,Zr,Hf,Ta) morphology, or directly links that network to crack deflection and plasticity compensation. Selecting this precise composition and morphology therefore still requires a substantial new design idea."
